WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:04.779
DANIELLE PLETKA: Good morning, everybody.
Welcome to the American Enterprise Institute.

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:09.780
I'm Danielle Pletka. I'm the senior vice president
for foreign and defense policy studies here

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:16.020
at AEI. And it is really an honor for us to
welcome the director of the Center Intelligence

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:22.119
Agency, Mike Pompeo, to AEI for I think the
first time in this current role - in this

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:23.380
current role.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.369
So I'm going to read your bio. This is something
that I don't always do. Those of you who know

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:31.380
me know that I tend to kind of race through.
But I sat down and looked at the director's

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.620
bio this morning, and I was really enormously
impressed. So I'm going to read it even and

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.750
embarrass you.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:45.890
So sworn in as director of the CIA in January
of 2017, Mr. Pompeo was a congressman on the

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.400
House Permanent Select Committee on Intelligence
and the Committee on Energy and Commerce prior

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.790
to that. He served on the House Select Benghazi
Committee. But before that, he was in business

00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:01.910
for himself. He headed up his own company,
and he was a graduate, the number one in his

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:07.200
class, from West Point. Served with the Fourth
ID and along the Iron Curtain when we still

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.189
had an Iron Curtain, although we're well on
our way back. And graduated after serving

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:19.119
from Harvard Law School, where he was the
head of the law review. That's pretty impressive.

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:24.079
So really an honor to have you serving in
our government and delighted to see it.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:28.040
Now, normally, I would give you a little bit
of a rundown on what the director was going

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:35.040
to say, but unusually perhaps in this administration,
I have absolutely no idea. No leaks out of

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.280
the CIA. What's happened? You've imposed a
kind of a discipline on everybody that is

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.689
just heretofore unknown.

00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:44.469
The director is going to come up here, he's
going to speak for a little while, and then

00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:49.950
he's going to sit down with AEI's own Marc
Thiessen for a conversation with Marc and

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.909
then some questions for the audience.

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:58.049
So, without further ado, Mr. Director.

00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:08.200
MIKE POMPEO: Good morning, everyone. Today
is in fact exactly one year - in a few hours,

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.650
it will be one year to the minute when I was
sworn in as the director of the CIA. It was

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.810
supposed to be the weekend before, but you
know how the Senate goes. I got held up across

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:18.840
the weekend.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.590
Danielle, thanks for the kind introduction.
Marc, thanks for having me here. I thought

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.610
I'd spend a few minutes this morning talking
about that year, that year from the Central

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:37.420
Intelligence Agency's perspective. You know,
it's been a very different role than I had,

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.060
although I did serve on the House Oversight
Committee as you describe, Danielle, so I

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:47.480
have had a chance to see the CIA as a member
of Congress. And I've told my friends that

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.930
are still on the committee, it is not possible
to gain a full - the full perspective of what

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:59.430
the US intelligence services collectively
can do until someone gets the incredible privilege

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:05.060
like the one that I have to lead one of those
organizations. Nothing can possibly prepare

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.980
you for the breadth, the depth, the scope,
the scale, the magnitude of the efforts that

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.850
we undertake each and every day as the nation's
first line of defense.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:22.870
You know, I go back to the president's inaugural
speech. He talked about this being the hour

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:30.030
for the American action. And, at the CIA,
I have tried to live that and have my team

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:36.910
live that. We're doers at CIA, trying to stay
on the right side of decorum, but we just

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:44.350
get stuff done. And I think if the American
people had the capacity to see that each and

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:49.070
every day - and they can't for good reason
- they too would be as proud of the men and

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.680
women who have joined the CIA as I am.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.880
I came to quickly understand too that I wasn't
going to improve the courage of our officers,

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:03.130
the skill set of our officers, although we're
working diligently to take it to even the

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:11.060
next level. But having led two small businesses,
having been a leader in the military, I could

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:16.759
see that there was a bureaucracy that was
preventing them from being unleashed - from

00:04:16.759 --> 00:04:21.079
doing the very things that they were directed,
commanded, and indeed America needed them

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:22.580
to do.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:28.990
And so I wanted to be part of changing that
understanding. I wanted to make sure our officers

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:34.759
appreciated the fact that we were going to
have an expectation nearly every day that

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:41.370
we were going to steal secrets. It's what
espionage services do. We ask our officers

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:47.539
to risk their lives to steal secrets to protect
America. It's our fundamental mission. We

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:54.160
will never shy away from it. And we do so
aggressively and without any apology.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.099
But it wasn't enough for me to talk about
it. We had to start to implement it. So we've

00:04:57.099 --> 00:05:02.580
tried to do that. One of the things that we
have done - you all have seen this in every

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.040
organization you've ever been a part of. It
is true in government, but it was true in

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:13.249
the private sector when I led there as well.
Bureaucracies just simply slow stuff down.

00:05:13.249 --> 00:05:17.440
Government's worse than the private sector
because the incentive systems are misaligned.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:24.199
And so I led by example. Forty percent of
the decisions that were previously made by

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:29.889
the director of the CIA no longer are made
by me. You might say, wow, that's reckless.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:35.790
I would tell you it was reckless to do it
the other way. We made careful decisions about

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:42.719
which pieces to keep. If it had significant
risk, cost, political, military, risked the

00:05:42.719 --> 00:05:47.230
lives of our officers, that's important for
the director to have. If the director brought

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:52.210
a special knowledge to bear, if I had an experience
set or if it needed full input from all of

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.800
the intelligence community or the broader
US government, I sit as a member of the cabinet,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.259
then I'd keep that decision.

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:03.419
But if it was coming to me just because I
was the next fellow on the chain of command,

00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:09.469
then that's a mistake because I would inevitably
slow it down and I would not be in the position

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:13.409
to add any value to that decision-making process.

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:20.110
I spent a minute talking about that, my role.
I have tried to impart that same thing everywhere

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.490
in our organization. I have asked every leader
to make sure and empower the people that work

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:30.100
for them, and I have tried to encourage the
people that work for them to go grab that

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:35.599
authority. If we do that, we'll be as fast
as our adversaries.

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:39.620
I've told this story once before. I was sitting
in a very long meeting with some very senior

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:45.629
officials, and I was asked, "Boy, if we did
X, what would our adversaries do?" And I responded

00:06:45.629 --> 00:06:51.719
by saying, "They sure as heck won't have a
meeting like this." (Laughs.) That's exactly

00:06:51.719 --> 00:06:57.000
how it was met in the meeting too, a little
laughter and some real concern. But everyone

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.949
knew I was right as well. We need to have
a bias towards being as nimble as our adversaries.

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:07.939
If we don't, we will serve America poorly,
and we won't steal the secrets that our president

00:07:07.939 --> 00:07:13.249
and our senior policymakers most need at the
most challenging times.

00:07:13.249 --> 00:07:18.599
Here's another good example. We were forced
by circumstances on the ground to close down

00:07:18.599 --> 00:07:24.999
one of our operations, a station. The team
came and said, "Here's what I think we're

00:07:24.999 --> 00:07:30.729
going to lose." We all found it unacceptable.
And I gave the commander's intent. I said,

00:07:30.729 --> 00:07:33.710
"We're going to be out, we're going to move
out of that station, we're going to set the

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:39.850
date, and we aren't going to lose a thing.
Go figure out a way to do it." And, remarkably

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:45.610
now, some months later, I can tell you that
our intelligence posture's actually equal

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:51.879
to where it was. We did some remarkable things,
some creative things, some things that absolutely

00:07:51.879 --> 00:08:00.110
had real risk and continue to, but it's in
a place and on a subject about an adversary

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:04.800
that we simply couldn't afford to have a gap
any larger than the one that we had before

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.539
that facility went away.

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:15.360
I remember too - I remember when Assad used
chemical weapons against his own people. The

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.820
president called me personally and said, "Mike,
I want to know what happened there." It took

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:24.369
us some number of hours before we could deliver
to the president a real response, a substantive

00:08:24.369 --> 00:08:29.909
response that could answer the key questions.
It was clear from what I'd heard from the

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:34.131
president he wanted to take action in response
to the chemical attack, but he needed to know

00:08:34.131 --> 00:08:38.769
it was the regime. He needed to know that
it was in fact chemical weapons that there

00:08:38.769 --> 00:08:41.029
were in fact used against civilians.

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:45.451
Those sound easy. Everyone could see the open-source
material, but we all know for a president

00:08:45.451 --> 00:08:50.769
to act, you need more than that. We've put
together a team that amounted to the hundreds

00:08:50.769 --> 00:08:57.050
that worked every intelligence channel. And
we were in just a short order able to deliver

00:08:57.050 --> 00:09:01.649
to the president the three basic facts he
needed to know with the certainty that I could

00:09:01.649 --> 00:09:05.670
stand in front of him and commit that he would
not - we would not later find that we were

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:10.620
wrong and that he had acted in error. And
we delivered it to him in a way that represented

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:14.570
the finest of what this agency does.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:18.750
If I can leave one thing behind as the director
- maybe next year I can stand here and tell

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:24.390
you that we've even made more progress - it
would be to be as agile and as speedy as we

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:29.430
need to. The second thing would be is to ensure
that we continue to keep the American people's

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:39.209
trust. It's in law. And as I was reading in
preparation for my confirmation hearing, it

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:44.520
continued to strike me how much power and
authority are granted to the director of the

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:52.220
Central Intelligence Agency and, through that,
to our remarkable officers.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:58.990
We have an obligation to do everything we
can to operate in a way that engenders the

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:03.990
American people's trust so that those power
and authorities remain in place. If we don't,

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:08.319
if we behave in ways that are lawless, if
we behave in ways that you might see in the

00:10:08.319 --> 00:10:14.120
movies, then the American people would rightfully
take those powers, that authority, that capacity

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:19.050
away from us. That would be unforgivable for
an agency to find itself in that place. What

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.959
we do is simply too important.

00:10:21.959 --> 00:10:27.690
I see it against whichever target we're working,
whether it's efforts to help the secretary

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:31.819
of state, the president to understand what's
going in North Korea, whether it's our efforts

00:10:31.819 --> 00:10:36.540
to counter Iranian influence throughout the
world, whether it's work we're doing against

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:44.290
Russia, those authorities, that trust that
the American people have provided to us are

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.670
central to us achieving our mission.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:51.250
And so days like today, which are few and
far between when I come out of Langley and

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:57.070
speak publicly, it's important for the American
people to understand that we operate inside

00:10:57.070 --> 00:11:03.670
a democracy, that we respect the rule of law
deeply, that we have processes in place to

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:09.720
ensure that we continue to do that, and that
we are working diligently to make sure that

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:14.560
the people entrusted with ensuring that's
happening in clandestine space, our oversight

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.980
committees and the executive branch in which
we work are fully informed of the things we're

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:24.490
doing. Everyone in my organization would know
that's my imperative, my directive for them,

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:28.190
and I believe we're doing that in a way that
the American people ought to be incredibly

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:33.170
proud of.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:37.880
There are a handful of stories - I may wait
for Marc to come up here, and I'll tell a

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:44.779
couple of them. But you should know that we
are focused on the same set of priorities

00:11:44.779 --> 00:11:48.231
that if you had the secretary of state or
the secretary of defense or the president

00:11:48.231 --> 00:11:55.440
standing before you today, we would be very
closely aligned. It's been in the news some.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:02.129
Last year, it was remarkable CIA creativity
which has now led to our capacity to materially

00:12:02.129 --> 00:12:07.779
impact the capacity of the American government
to interdict shipments into North Korea. We're

00:12:07.779 --> 00:12:14.399
not quite where we need to be. Our mission
is not complete, but we have officers all

00:12:14.399 --> 00:12:19.889
around the world working diligently to make
sure that we do everything we can to support

00:12:19.889 --> 00:12:25.170
the US pressure campaign and to tighten the
sanctions in such a way that we have the opportunity

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:31.889
to prevail and achieve the American president's
mission, which is the denuclearization of

00:12:31.889 --> 00:12:38.040
the peninsula. This is the kind of task that
the CIA was designed for. It's the kind of

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:42.550
task we're delivering against.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:49.860
I talked last week about the fact that North
Korea's ever closer to being able to hold

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:54.579
America at risk. I said there was a handful
of months. I had said the same thing several

00:12:54.579 --> 00:13:00.100
months before that. I want everyone to understand
that we are working diligently to make sure

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:05.480
that a year from now I can still tell you
they are several months away from having that

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:10.500
capacity. It's not a static time frame. There
is much effort all across the United States

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:16.839
government to ensure that Americans don't
have to feel at risk. We saw what happened

00:13:16.839 --> 00:13:21.550
in Hawaii. It is an imperative - an American
national imperative that we as an intelligence

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:26.300
agency deliver the information to our senior
leaders such that they can resolve this issue

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.540
in a way that works for the American people.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:37.579
Look, I'll pause here and take questions from
Marc and others. As we move into 2018, I want

00:13:37.579 --> 00:13:41.230
you all to know that we're going to continue
to do remarkable things on behalf of the American

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:46.370
people. We aren't just focused on North Korea
and Iran. We're working diligently to solve

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:54.620
problems in Venezuela and problems in Africa.
Our mission set is broad. The counterterrorism

00:13:54.620 --> 00:14:00.699
fight, which I have not yet mentioned, continues.
CIA is a central part of making sure that

00:14:00.699 --> 00:14:05.690
policymakers understand the threat and how
best to attack it.

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:11.160
Frankly, I have to tell you, I came in looking
at several of these problems and realized

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:16.699
that, frankly, we had been whistling past
the graveyard for decades on some of these.

00:14:16.699 --> 00:14:20.769
And you should know that when I say decades,
that is Republican presidents, Democrat presidents,

00:14:20.769 --> 00:14:25.589
Republican Congresses of which I was a member
and members of - Democrats who were members

00:14:25.589 --> 00:14:31.910
of our legislative branch as well. Some of
these need to be taken off the table. We need

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:38.019
to reduce this risk, and CIA is prepared to
do its part to ensure that those risks are

00:14:38.019 --> 00:14:45.089
reduced and that we ultimately can stare at
these problems with fewer resources consumed

00:14:45.089 --> 00:14:47.629
because we have actually resolved many of
them.

00:14:47.629 --> 00:14:54.060
Marc, I look forward to our conversation.
And thank you all for being here today.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:15:03.889
MARC THIESSEN: Thank you, Mike, for being
here at AEI. You've been a longtime friend

00:15:03.889 --> 00:15:05.970
of the organization, and so we're proud to
have you here -

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:06.970
MR. POMPEO: Thanks.

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:09.930
MR. THIESSEN: - as one of your rare public
appearances. So there's -

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:13.569
MR. POMPEO: I see all these people I haven't
seen for a long time. How are you all doing?

00:15:13.569 --> 00:15:14.569
(Laughs.)

00:15:14.569 --> 00:15:19.350
MR. THIESSEN: So we've had a lot of people
without firsthand knowledge commentating and

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:24.509
writing books and otherwise about the president's
briefing practices. You brief the president

00:15:24.509 --> 00:15:29.740
almost every day as part of the presidential
daily brief, so I'd like you, as someone with

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.870
that firsthand knowledge, to take us inside
the - and spend a little time on this - take

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:37.311
us inside the president's daily brief. How
does it work? How does he receive it? What

00:15:37.311 --> 00:15:41.640
kind of a consumer of intelligence is he?

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:49.850
MR. POMPEO: So nearly every day, I get up,
get ready, read the material that's been presented

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:54.899
early in the morning, and then trundle down
from Langley to the White House. We present

00:15:54.899 --> 00:15:58.949
the president with the most exquisite information
any policymaker would ever have the privilege

00:15:58.949 --> 00:16:03.850
of having a chance to read. That's the mission
set every day for the analysts that prepare

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:08.290
the book. And it is. It's quality material.

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:13.730
I'm there. General McMaster is there. Director
Coates is there. I have a professional briefer

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:18.940
with me as well, a CIA officer who attends
the briefing as well. The vice president's

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:24.029
there when he's in town. That's the gang that's
usually assembled before the president.

00:16:24.029 --> 00:16:31.060
Someone shouts, "Pompeo, you're in," and I
take a deep breath. And then we deliver it

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:37.069
to him with three buckets really so we will
try - and this doesn't happen each day because

00:16:37.069 --> 00:16:42.740
the world is too varied, but each day we try
to do something that's of the moment; that

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:46.990
is, we'll talk about things that happened
overnight. For instance, today, you can imagine

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:52.449
we would have talked about what's taking place
in Afrin, Turkey, the Turks moving south out

00:16:52.449 --> 00:16:53.449
of Syria.

00:16:53.449 --> 00:17:00.700
And then we'll try also to talk about something
that is coming up. So, for instance, preparing

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:05.850
the president for his trip to Davos or a foreign
leader who's coming to visit. Or provide him

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:10.679
with material that we know he's going to confront
in the days or weeks ahead. And then we create

00:17:10.679 --> 00:17:17.909
some space as well - some space to do knowledge
building for the team, strategic items, things

00:17:17.909 --> 00:17:21.329
that wouldn't be confronted - that won't be
in the news tonight or next week, but that

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:28.350
we know are central to having a shared fact-based
understanding across all of the agency. And

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:31.270
so those would be the three types of information.

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:37.430
The president asks hard questions. He's deeply
engaged. We'll have rambunctious back and

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:42.010
forth, all aimed at making sure we're delivering
him the truth as best we understand it. He'll

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:45.280
ask questions from time to time that we frankly
don't have the answer to. We didn't bring

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:50.840
it, or we just - weren't as complete as we
need it to be. We'll go back and within a

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:58.779
couple of hours deliver that information as
best we can. It is a process - the process

00:17:58.779 --> 00:18:03.220
that we go through with the president each
day is a process that it is my hope every

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:08.290
senior policymaker is doing with the various
briefers that we have throughout the administration.

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:12.350
I hope they're all consuming the information
that we're delivering. We spend a lot of money

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.900
on it in the same way that the president does.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:18.480
MR. THIESSEN: You said you had some stories.
And we had talked earlier whether there was

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:22.909
the possibility you could declassify a few
things that had been discussed in the PDB.

00:18:22.909 --> 00:18:27.480
But is there an example or any examples of
times where the president has pushed back

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:32.340
on you and pushed you to get more information
and you've been able to change the outcome

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:37.080
of something or, you know - and I know this
is the most sensitive debriefing there is.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:42.590
MR. POMPEO: I'll give you a couple of examples.
The president was very concerned about the

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:47.299
humanitarian issues taking place in Yemen,
the risk of cholera and starvation that was

00:18:47.299 --> 00:18:51.120
taking place. And he kept pushing us about
what was really taking place on hand. What

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:55.950
was the real layout? What was happening in
the port? What was possible given the configuration

00:18:55.950 --> 00:19:02.049
of forces on the ground? And he pushed us
a couple -three days until we were able to

00:19:02.049 --> 00:19:06.160
deliver him a satisfactory picture where he
then could make a decision about which of

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:11.500
our friends to call to try and make sure that
that problem was at least diminished or mitigated.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:17.730
And there's a second example I remember, it
was a little bit before that. It was on Venezuela.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:24.799
The president was dissatisfied with the description
of the situation as we had laid it out for

00:19:24.799 --> 00:19:30.000
him. And so we kept coming back. It was some
financial issues he wanted more clarity on:

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.620
Who had the money, where was the debt, what
was the timing of that? There were multiple

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:39.240
pieces. The array of the Maduro and his forces
- he wanted to really understand how they

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:46.250
all came together so he could have a complete
picture, and it wasn't long thereafter that

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:48.930
the - it would have been the first or second
set of sanctions that the administration put

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:53.960
in place were enabled by the very intelligence
that we had delivered and he had requested.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:57.460
MR. THIESSEN: So, as you know - I mean, different
presidents take the presidential daily brief

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:01.870
differently. President Obama didn't take it
in person more than half the time. And it

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:05.700
seemed like during the transition that President
Trump was going to sort of follow that model.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.950
He said, you know, why do I need to take - get
the same people telling me the same thing

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:11.000
over and over again for every day for eight
years of my presidency.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:12.059
MR. POMPEO: Yeah. We try to avoid that.

00:20:12.059 --> 00:20:16.409
MR. THIESSEN: And so what are his - that's
obviously changed. So how often does he take

00:20:16.409 --> 00:20:21.350
the presidential daily brief? Is it daily?
And what are his briefing habits?

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:27.950
MR. POMPEO: It's not daily, but it's near
daily. It happened today. It happened yesterday.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:31.559
It will happen most days. It depends on the
president's schedule. We'll often do it when

00:20:31.559 --> 00:20:36.110
he's traveling, too, although I don't travel
to deliver that myself. We'll have a briefer

00:20:36.110 --> 00:20:43.409
provide it to him. It's scheduled for about
half an hour. Often goes on for 40 minutes

00:20:43.409 --> 00:20:45.330
or so, depending on the president's schedule.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:48.590
MR. THIESSEN: How did he become convinced
of the need for it?

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:55.060
MR. POMPEO: I think it's about value. I think
in life we all end up with our schedules shaped

00:20:55.060 --> 00:21:00.110
by things that are proving valuable in executing
our jobs. And so the meetings that I take

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:04.130
today are very different than the meetings
that my predecessor took. My battle rhythm

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:09.929
is different than his. This president's pattern
of taking this information is different than

00:21:09.929 --> 00:21:13.549
President Obama's. I've done a little bit
of historical reading. It's different than

00:21:13.549 --> 00:21:15.399
President Clinton's as well.

00:21:15.399 --> 00:21:20.110
And I think the reason he does it is because
he finds value. That is, we're able to convince

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:25.690
him that the facts we're delivering impact
his capacity to perform his mission. I think

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:29.260
the day that we can't - I think the day that
we can't deliver that would be the day that

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:33.919
it starts getting pushed off and other things
begin to occupy that time and space. I tell

00:21:33.919 --> 00:21:39.110
my team as they're preparing for what it is
we're going to substantively brief - we have

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:45.539
a big process, an elaborate process that picks
those topics - we have to make sure that the

00:21:45.539 --> 00:21:49.840
information we're delivering meets the threshold
for the president of the United States and

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:56.470
is delivered in a manner which he can grasp
sufficiently to actually be able to act upon

00:21:56.470 --> 00:22:01.409
- to provide real value, not just data, not
just some set of random facts but real data

00:22:01.409 --> 00:22:05.640
that he can use to formulate policy. If we
do that, I'm convinced we'll continue the

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.590
pattern that I think is serving America well
today.

00:22:08.590 --> 00:22:13.010
MR. THIESSEN: How do you see him as a consumer
of intelligence? Is he a sophisticated consumer

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:20.110
of intelligence? And what do his - your interactions
with him in that setting tell you about him

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:21.110
as a commander-in-chief?

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:31.240
MR. POMPEO: I have seen 25-year intelligence
professionals receive briefings. I would tell

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:36.679
you that President Trump is the kind of recipient
of our information at the same level that

00:22:36.679 --> 00:22:41.670
they are. Look, I learn a lot every day, too.
I still - you can ask me about certain places

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:45.700
in the world I need to get up to speed, right?
None of us come with an encyclopedic knowledge

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:50.460
of the world, and the world moves fast enough
that what we knew yesterday or a year ago

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:57.630
is of only modest interest today. Things move
awfully fast. He has the grounding for him

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:01.799
to be able to grasp this information in a
way that he can ask sophisticated questions

00:23:01.799 --> 00:23:04.840
that then lead to important policy discussions.

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:10.330
I watch it, Marc. We'll be sitting in a National
Security Council meeting talking about a particular

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:14.809
topic, and he'll bring up something that I
briefed him on weeks or months ago. It could

00:23:14.809 --> 00:23:19.770
be that he knew that before that. I'm going
to take full credit for having been the source

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:23.870
of that knowledge, but I've seen this time
and time again. So it's not simply the case

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:29.220
that this is an exercise. He's using it. He's
taking it on board, and I'm confident that

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:34.650
our team is delivering in a way that's delivering
value to the president and to not just him,

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.720
but to our senior policymakers as well.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:41.380
MR. THIESSEN: Is the fact that so many people
underestimate him actually a valuable asset

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:45.429
in terms of national security? It's often
said, you know, people underestimated George

00:23:45.429 --> 00:23:49.960
W. Bush, a C student from Yale and all the
rest, that he wasn't that smart. And he was

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:55.899
actually a very smart man. Is that a useful
tool in national security that people are

00:23:55.899 --> 00:23:56.899
underestimating him?

00:23:56.899 --> 00:24:04.130
MR. POMPEO: I don't know. Lots of us have
been underestimated many times. Just keep

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:05.130
plugging.

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:10.220
MR. THIESSEN: All right. Well, let's talk
about your leadership at the CIA. You recently

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:14.809
said that your goal is to make the CIA more
vicious, more aggressive, more inclined to

00:24:14.809 --> 00:24:19.309
take risks to come directly at the threats
that America faces in the world. How are you

00:24:19.309 --> 00:24:23.980
making the CIA more vicious, more aggressive,
and less risk averse in the fight against

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:24.980
terror?

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:29.000
MR. POMPEO: It's all about incentives. It's
all about the things that are rewarded. In

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:35.020
every organization I've been a part of, it
is the human condition that you respond to

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:39.630
the guidance, to commander's intent, the incentives
that are laid out in an organization.

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:46.360
So you used CT as an example. It's important.
If you say it's a priority, you'd better mean

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:49.559
it, and by mean it doesn't mean - it doesn't
mean just giving really good speeches about

00:24:49.559 --> 00:24:53.700
it. It means if you care about something,
you will apply resources against it. In the

00:24:53.700 --> 00:24:59.059
agency, that's money, people, technical skill,
other tools that we have. You will actually

00:24:59.059 --> 00:25:02.289
take those and apply them against that problem
set.

00:25:02.289 --> 00:25:06.259
When you operate in a constrained environment
- that is, we have a finite set of resources

00:25:06.259 --> 00:25:12.559
- that means you have to deprioritize something
else as well. It's almost impossible to avoid

00:25:12.559 --> 00:25:17.090
that. And we've done it. We have said, hey,
here are the things that matter most to us.

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:22.049
Reprioritize and we have to reshuffle that.
People see that and people then want to go

00:25:22.049 --> 00:25:27.470
to the place where they believe the mission
set is, at least CIA officers are very much

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:29.890
that way. And so we've done that. We have
said, hey, here are the things that we're

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:33.799
going to work diligently against, and here's
the outcomes that we're going to deliver for

00:25:33.799 --> 00:25:37.210
the president, for the country. And we have
prioritized that way.

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:42.690
The second thing we've done - Danielle talked
about this a little bit - I ran a company

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:46.659
that sold equipment to oil and gas industry
before I came to Congress. It was the last

00:25:46.659 --> 00:25:54.230
job that I had before I ran. I worked with
companies that were drilling for oil and natural

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:57.360
gas - best companies in the world, smartest
engineers, most talented people, and they

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:03.870
drilled dry holes all the time. They didn't
cry. They didn't punish the engineer who'd

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:06.870
made that choice or that selection. They got
up the next morning, tried to do a little

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:11.380
bit better to make sure the next one returned
something. I want to create that culture here

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:12.380
at CIA too.

00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:15.480
If we're going to do it right, if we're going
to do this well, we're going to have failed

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:21.210
missions. It is inevitable. Almost by definition
- I'm an engineer by training - almost by

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:26.049
definition, if you move out on the risk profile,
you will increase the number of times you

00:26:26.049 --> 00:26:32.409
will have failure. We're going to do that.
And we're going to make sure that people aren't

00:26:32.409 --> 00:26:38.419
punished for that, but they are rather recognized
for having been professional, for having operated

00:26:38.419 --> 00:26:42.990
against the target set and having done something
incredibly audacious. And if it turns out

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:48.159
the coin just ends up tails instead of heads,
so be it. We're going to go next day and go

00:26:48.159 --> 00:26:50.269
crush our adversary one more time.

00:26:50.269 --> 00:26:54.400
MR. THIESSEN: That's great. Last time you
were here at AEI for when we had this kind

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:57.520
of a conversation, the topic was Guantanamo.
You were a congressman. You had just returned

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:00.760
from Guantanamo Bay, and you made the point
that far from closing -

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:02.659
MR. POMPEO: It seems like a long time ago.

00:27:02.659 --> 00:27:05.990
MR. THIESSEN: It does, yeah. But you made
the point that apart from closing it, we ought

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:10.350
to be filling it with new people. When are
we going to get some new residents at Guantanamo

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:11.529
Bay? (Laughs.)

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:15.870
MR. POMPEO: Well, I can't answer that. One
of the glories of being the CIA director is

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:22.610
you're out of the policy world. Here's what
I - from my perspective now, here's what I

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:28.419
can say. For the CIA, what is important is
that if we're going to take down networks,

00:27:28.419 --> 00:27:35.610
we need the opportunity to engage with individuals
whom have been pulled from the battlefield.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:41.120
We need to make sure we have the time, the
space, the capacity to take onboard the information

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:43.260
that these individuals may well possess.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:50.570
And so US government policy, if we are serious
about these fights, must reflect that. We

00:27:50.570 --> 00:27:55.309
have to make sure that not just the CIA, but
all - but DOD and all of the others who have

00:27:55.309 --> 00:28:00.909
a part in the counterterrorism fight have
that opportunity. And so I'm working diligently

00:28:00.909 --> 00:28:06.570
inside the administration to make sure that
we have that. How that will play out, how

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:12.140
Congress will - Congress will definitely have
its say here, right? Today we have a set of

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:16.700
rules for interrogation purposes. We have
the Army Field Manual for detention purposes.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:24.140
DOD has the authority. That seems fine to
me. But the moment I have officers come to

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:28.269
me and say, "Well, we missed something. We
missed an opportunity to conduct an interview

00:28:28.269 --> 00:28:32.429
on someone who I believe had information that
could save an American life," we're going

00:28:32.429 --> 00:28:36.130
to begin to move heaven and earth to make
sure that something like that does not ever

00:28:36.130 --> 00:28:37.130
happen again.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:42.669
MR. THIESSEN: So since President Obama ended
the CIA's interrogation program, we've captured

00:28:42.669 --> 00:28:49.149
and interrogated very few people. We've relied
more and more on signals intelligence. But

00:28:49.149 --> 00:28:54.340
our signals capability has been decimated
by leaks in recent years. And your predecessor,

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:59.090
Mike Hayden, pointed out recently that in
an age of end-to-end encryption, which is

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:05.299
increasingly what we're getting, signals intelligence
is going to be getting less content and more

00:29:05.299 --> 00:29:11.169
following what he calls digital exhaust. Can
we keep the country safe without content?

00:29:11.169 --> 00:29:14.919
And doesn't that suggest that we need to start
getting human intelligence again in a way

00:29:14.919 --> 00:29:15.940
we haven't been?

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:18.430
MR. POMPEO: A lot of predicates in your question.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:19.430
MR. THIESSEN: Yeah.

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:26.370
MR. POMPEO: And I love General Hayden. He's
a dear friend. We're still doing pretty good

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:30.510
collecting signals intelligence, mostly our
partner, not the CIA, but we have a small

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:37.120
role in that as well. But that does not foreclose
the absolute imperative that we continue to

00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:41.580
improve our capacity to collect human intelligence.
That includes human intelligence through lots

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:46.600
of different forms, not the least of which
is the capacity to interview those folks who

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:52.880
have been pulled from the battlefields. So
there's lots of ways to collect human intelligence.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:58.091
We should not put ourselves in a position
where we are - we're making decisions on the

00:29:58.091 --> 00:30:03.649
assumption that we can't detain an interview
to improve America's information center.

00:30:03.649 --> 00:30:06.600
MR. THIESSEN: So the Trump administration
- let's talk about North Korea. The Trump

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:11.519
administration obviously inherited a mess
in North Korea that, as you pointed out, has

00:30:11.519 --> 00:30:16.649
gone - in both Republican and Democratic administration
preceding it so you could argue that for the

00:30:16.649 --> 00:30:20.289
last 25 years, kicked the can down the road
on North Korea, and now we're out of road

00:30:20.289 --> 00:30:23.230
and this is coming to a head.

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:29.190
How does North Korea's missile program jump
from a dozen unsuccessful tests a year ago

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:34.279
to the state that it's in right now? And is
that alarming in the sense that - are they

00:30:34.279 --> 00:30:38.269
getting at a build rate where they can actually
will be able to overpower our ballistic missile

00:30:38.269 --> 00:30:40.210
defense capability? And what do we do about
that?

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:43.250
MR. POMPEO: So I can't share much with you
about this.

00:30:43.250 --> 00:30:44.269
MR. THIESSEN: OK.

00:30:44.269 --> 00:30:48.820
MR. POMPEO: Other than to say this: They have
moved at a very rapid clip, make no mistake

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:56.380
about it. Their testing capacity has improved,
and the frequency that they have tests, which

00:30:56.380 --> 00:31:01.890
are materially successful, has also improved,
putting them ever closer to a place where

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:03.633
Americans can be held at risk. I think that's
a true statement.

00:31:03.633 --> 00:31:12.100
It is also analytically true that Kim Jong
Un will not rest with a single successful

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:18.460
test, right? The logical next step would be
to develop an arsenal of weapons; that is,

00:31:18.460 --> 00:31:24.519
not one, not a showpiece, not something to
drive on a parade route on February 8, but

00:31:24.519 --> 00:31:33.419
rather the capacity to deliver from multiple
firings of these missiles simultaneously.

00:31:33.419 --> 00:31:37.919
And that increases the risk to America, and
that's the very mission set that President

00:31:37.919 --> 00:31:42.039
Trump has directed the government to figure
out a way to make sure it never occurs.

00:31:42.039 --> 00:31:46.549
MR. THIESSEN: Does the CIA assess that Kim
Jong Un is a rational actor?

00:31:46.549 --> 00:31:48.630
MR. POMPEO: We do. We do.

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:54.370
MR. THIESSEN: OK. And do you think that he
believes that the Trump administration is

00:31:54.370 --> 00:31:58.340
actually willing to use military force, because
the only way you can have a successful diplomatic

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:04.399
solution is if he feels the threat. And he
seems to perceive that he's untouchable because

00:32:04.399 --> 00:32:09.010
of what he can do to Seoul and what - even
his conventional capability, much less his

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:13.940
emerging nuclear capability. Does he really
believe that we'd pull the trigger and do

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.169
something to threaten him?

00:32:16.169 --> 00:32:22.830
MR. POMPEO: So we're concerned that he may
not be getting really good, accurate information.

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:27.580
It is not a healthy thing to be a senior leader
and bring bad news to Kim Jong Un. Tell someone

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:35.169
you're going to do that, and try to get life
insurance. I dare you. (Laughs.)

00:32:35.169 --> 00:32:41.730
So we are doing - we're taking the real-world
actions that we think will make unmistakable

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:47.920
to Kim Jong Un that we are intent on denuclearization.
We're counting on the fact that he'll see

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:55.750
it. We're confident that he will. And then,
we will continue to have discussions about

00:32:55.750 --> 00:32:57.970
how to achieve that denuclearization.

00:32:57.970 --> 00:33:04.429
MR. THIESSEN: Can we live in a world where
Kim Jong Un has the capability to destroy

00:33:04.429 --> 00:33:08.840
New York or Washington with a push of a button?
I mean, is that a world that we're willing

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:09.840
to go into?

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:14.710
MR. POMPEO: So that will be a decision for
the president ultimately. I think he has been

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:17.080
unambiguous about his view on that.

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:23.950
MR. THIESSEN: Is he deterrable? Is Kim deterrable,
or does he want these weapons for another

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:27.590
purpose? This is the debate within the people
who have watched Korea is that does he want

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:31.790
this for regime preservation. Or does he want
it because it gives him the freedom of action

00:33:31.790 --> 00:33:33.960
to do things that are destabilizing in the
region?

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:40.549
MR. POMPEO: So we've said - the CIA has said
this publicly before. It is more than just

00:33:40.549 --> 00:33:45.870
regime preservation that we are concerned
he will use. We talk about the nuclear risk

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:51.100
all the time. That gets the brunt of the world's
attention and appropriately so, but his conventional

00:33:51.100 --> 00:33:56.950
forces alone, right, close to a million under
arms, depending on how you count them, is

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:59.289
no small thing.

00:33:59.289 --> 00:34:03.710
And so we do believe that Kim Jong Un, given
these tool sets would use them for things

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:08.659
besides simply regime protection; that is,
to put pressure on what is his ultimate goal,

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:15.040
which is reunification of the peninsula under
his authority. And so we don't think it's

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:20.190
the case that he's simply going to use this
tool set for self-preservation. We think he'll

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:24.340
use it in a way that is either - call it what
you will, call it coercive - is perhaps the

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:29.510
best way to think about how Kim Jong Un is
prepared to potentially use these weapons.

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:35.770
MR. THIESSEN: There seems to be a perception
that the options in North Korea are nuclear

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:41.779
war or going to a deterrence strategy. And
there are options in between that. I mean,

00:34:41.779 --> 00:34:48.149
if he's a rational actor and President Trump
decided to do something like a limited strike,

00:34:48.149 --> 00:34:53.149
like the one he had done in Syria, would - a
rational actor would not respond the way the

00:34:53.149 --> 00:34:57.690
Syrian regime didn't respond, right, in the
sense that it would lead to regime destruction.

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:02.910
So if you're a rational actor who wants to
preserve your regime, are there options to

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:07.970
address his capability militarily that don't
involve a nuclear war between the United States

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:08.970
and North Korea?

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:12.270
MR. POMPEO: I'm thrilled that you asked. I'm
equally happy not to answer. (Laughter.)

00:35:12.270 --> 00:35:15.320
MR. THIESSEN: Good. All right. And, by the
way, I have to ask, and we all understand

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:17.270
when you can't answer. One -

00:35:17.270 --> 00:35:22.670
MR. POMPEO: I might say this though. The American
people should know, we're working to prepare

00:35:22.670 --> 00:35:26.520
a series of options to make sure that we can
deliver a range of things so the president

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:31.300
will have the full suite of possibilities.
The president is intent on delivering this

00:35:31.300 --> 00:35:37.150
solution through diplomatic means. It is the
focus. It has been uniformly that for now

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:43.849
365 days. It remains so today. We are focused
like a laser on achieving that. We are equally,

00:35:43.849 --> 00:35:48.370
at the same time, ensuring that the - if we
conclude that it is not possible, that we

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:53.119
present the president with a range of options
that can achieve what is his stated intention.

00:35:53.119 --> 00:35:56.920
MR. THIESSEN: OK. Let's talk about Iran and
sort of related to North Korea. I mean, one

00:35:56.920 --> 00:36:03.770
of the holes in the nuclear deal is old-school
compartmentalization in the sense that - assumption

00:36:03.770 --> 00:36:08.770
that all the work on the country's nuclear
program occurs within the country. To what

00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:13.970
extent could Iran use its - we know that there
was North Korean cooperation with Syria, for

00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:18.640
example, in building that nuclear facility
that the Israelis took out. To what extent

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:22.490
could Iran use its nuclear cooperation with
North Korea to conduct illicit nuclear work

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:27.491
like on warhead design that would advance
the - with Iranian scientists in North Korea

00:36:27.491 --> 00:36:31.720
that could advance their program without necessarily
our catching it or violating the JCPOA.

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:37.000
MR. POMPEO: It's a real risk. We think we
have a pretty good understanding of what's

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:42.660
taking place there today. Having said that,
I am the first person to admit that intelligence

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:48.550
organizations can miss important information.
These are terribly difficult problems in incredibly

00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:54.030
tight spaces. And when you're moving information,
sometimes difficult to detect that information

00:36:54.030 --> 00:37:00.010
is moved. And so if someone asks me, "As the
senior intelligence leader of the CIA, can

00:37:00.010 --> 00:37:04.750
you guarantee this?" I would say, "Absolutely
not." But we're working diligently to make

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:05.970
sure that doesn't happen.

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:09.440
This actually harkens back, Marc, to your
previous question about one of the risks of

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:13.470
allowing the North Korean regime to continue
to have this nuclear capability. It is this

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:20.710
proliferation risk. It is that this technology
that they have developed and then figured

00:37:20.710 --> 00:37:26.550
out how to manufacture at something beyond
just a museum piece - that is, some form of

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:31.910
production level of their capacity - would
then be proliferated elsewhere in the world.

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:37.670
And then, secondarily, it doesn't take too
much imagination to understand that if they

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:41.880
continue to have that nuclear weapon system
or if the Iranians make advancements in theirs,

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:49.010
that many other countries around the world
will decide "me too," right - that I want

00:37:49.010 --> 00:37:54.380
to have one of those things that that guy
has, being very careful not to identify countries.

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:58.220
But you can go through the list of those who
would feel incredibly threatened and feel

00:37:58.220 --> 00:38:03.230
that they needed to have a similar capacity
in order to defend their own national security

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:04.230
interests.

00:38:04.230 --> 00:38:09.119
MR. THIESSEN: Sure. Let's talk about Vladimir
Putin for a second. So this is a guy who,

00:38:09.119 --> 00:38:15.990
you know, who shows up a lot with his shirt
off, right? There's a famous story where he

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:23.300
saw Barney and told President Bush, "This
is my dog, bigger, stronger," you know. Are

00:38:23.300 --> 00:38:27.270
these behaviors of someone who's a strong
leader or a weak leader?

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:34.130
MR. POMPEO: You know, our assessment of Vladimir
Putin's intentions haven't changed. He continues

00:38:34.130 --> 00:38:40.180
to view the greatest failure of the last century
to have been the dissolution of the Soviet

00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:47.700
Union. He is bent on returning the former
Soviet Union to its greatness and glory. That's

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:52.610
what he wakes up thinking about each day.
You might add being reelected as the second

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:57.570
thing he thinks about each day. Those are
related in some ways; that is, I think his

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:01.710
- he moves about the world, he is conveying
back to his domestic audience the imperial

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:09.329
power of the Russian people. He hasn't changed.
This administration is deeply aware that we

00:39:09.329 --> 00:39:13.680
need to continue to push back against the
Russians everywhere we find them.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:19.420
MR. THIESSEN: So let's talk a little bit about
the recent news stories about - there was

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:24.060
a possible mole within the CIA that resulted
in the loss of a lot of Chinese assets in

00:39:24.060 --> 00:39:29.550
recent years, and it's hearkened back to losses
we had - you were stationed on the Iron Curtain.

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:34.391
We had losses of agents in the Soviet Union.
We've had a dozen or so Iranian recruits - this

00:39:34.391 --> 00:39:38.040
is public information - in the Middle East
who have been lost.

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:41.920
The business of recruiting and running spies
is hard, but the record suggests that we might

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:45.680
not be as proficient at it as we ought to
be. Is this something that needs to get fixed?

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:49.220
Are we doing well? How do you assess where
we are in terms of that?

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:54.270
MR. POMPEO: We're never where we need to be.
I actually saw this as a member of Congress.

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:59.869
I came in as the director a year ago intent
on improving our capacity to protect our own

00:39:59.869 --> 00:40:05.590
information. We should make sure that the
secrets we steal aren't re-stolen. We have

00:40:05.590 --> 00:40:07.599
an obligation to the American people to do
that.

00:40:07.599 --> 00:40:12.250
I've made a number of changes, one of which
is to make sure that we're providing the information

00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:18.880
so that the Department of Justice can do its
good work at bringing these traitors to heel

00:40:18.880 --> 00:40:24.410
in US courts. The second of which is making
sure that our organization has the resources

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:30.090
it needs to deliver on its counterintelligence
mission, which includes ensuring that we're

00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:34.880
doing offensive counterintelligence; that
is, working against our adversary's services

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:39.440
in a way that prevents them from getting inside
of our service.

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:44.210
One of the first things I did - the woman
who runs out counterintelligence mission center

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:50.640
reports directly to me now. That was intentional,
sending a signal to two places: one, to our

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:55.200
adversaries, that the CIA is going to be serious
about protecting our stuff, and second, to

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:59.589
my workforce - you know, that the director
was personally attentive to a mission that

00:40:59.589 --> 00:41:05.700
can fall too far down in the priority scheme.
To me, there are few things more important

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:09.900
than protecting our officers, our assets,
and our information.

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:16.150
MR. THIESSEN: It's also really hard to penetrate
groups like al Qaeda and ISIS. I mean, your

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:21.700
experience serving on the front lines of the
Cold War - you had Russian émigrés. It wasn't

00:41:21.700 --> 00:41:27.369
a tribal culture in the same way. I mean,
how hard is it to - before 9/11, we had almost

00:41:27.369 --> 00:41:33.440
no human assets. I assume we're doing better
in that way, but we've got - as al Qaeda was

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:38.970
pushed back, then all of a sudden ISIS emerged.
How hard is it to get human intelligence on

00:41:38.970 --> 00:41:41.089
these terrorist networks, the Salafi Jihadi
movement?

00:41:41.089 --> 00:41:47.410
MR. POMPEO: So they're difficult targets for
sure, but as the US government has been successful

00:41:47.410 --> 00:41:52.079
against them in different places, whether
it was a significant set of successes in the

00:41:52.079 --> 00:41:56.819
previous administration against al Qaeda,
as we've had significant success at taking

00:41:56.819 --> 00:42:03.300
the caliphate away against ISIS, it provides
us real opportunities to reach in. There are

00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:09.900
more people who decide being part of Team
America might be better than being part of

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:14.829
Team Jihadi. And so we are beneficiaries when
those big disruptions occur and they allow

00:42:14.829 --> 00:42:19.910
us to collect in ways that we can't when their
force is united and we don't have any chance

00:42:19.910 --> 00:42:20.910
to touch them.

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:25.810
MR. THIESSEN: So the success in Syria against
ISIS has been remarkable in terms of taking

00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:31.339
away their physical caliphate, but it's arguably
come at a little bit of a price because we

00:42:31.339 --> 00:42:36.430
did it with Kurdish fighters, which has caused
some tension with Turkey. We are perceived,

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:41.980
at least by the Sunni population in Syria,
as being at least tacitly in an alliance with

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:46.839
Russia and Iran in that fight against ISIS,
which pushes Sunnis away from us and toward

00:42:46.839 --> 00:42:49.260
al Qaeda, which is sitting there waiting.

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:55.040
Have we been too focused on ISIS and not focused
enough on al Qaeda? And can we defeat the

00:42:55.040 --> 00:43:01.440
global jihadi movement without bringing Sunnis
into our orbit? Can we do this with Kurds

00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:05.840
and Russians and Iranians, or do we need a
Sunni partner on the ground that's going to

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:06.840
fight these guys?

00:43:06.840 --> 00:43:10.540
MR. POMPEO: We absolutely need Sunni partners.
And I think we're working diligently to do

00:43:10.540 --> 00:43:14.890
that. In Eastern Syria, the Department of
Defense has done good work trying to bring

00:43:14.890 --> 00:43:21.030
the Sunnis in alongside our Kurdish partners
there as well. I think they've made real progress

00:43:21.030 --> 00:43:25.960
there. This administration has broadly reached
out to Sunni countries all throughout the

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:33.510
Middle East to form coalitions against - not
only against ISIS, but against Iran as well.

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:38.560
And so I think we have made some substantial
progress there. If we're going to be successful

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:42.490
at taking down the jihadist threat, we will
absolutely need Sunni partners aiding us in

00:43:42.490 --> 00:43:43.490
that effort.

00:43:43.490 --> 00:43:46.119
MR. THIESSEN: What worked in Iraq during the
surge was the sons of Iraq.

00:43:46.119 --> 00:43:47.119
MR. POMPEO: Right.

00:43:47.119 --> 00:43:52.099
MR. THIESSEN: The fact that the Sunni tribes
came over. We were a force enabler by sending

00:43:52.099 --> 00:43:57.630
additional troops, but it was really a Sunni
uprising against al Qaeda, which was both

00:43:57.630 --> 00:44:02.069
a military defeat and an ideological defeat
because the Jihadists claim to be the vanguard

00:44:02.069 --> 00:44:06.619
of the Sunni masses and so when they're rejected
by the Sunnis, it sends a signal throughout

00:44:06.619 --> 00:44:12.900
the region. How are we doing? Are we making
any progress in getting a sons of Iraq equivalent

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:16.490
in Syria and in some of these other places
where we're fighting them?

00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:20.020
MR. POMPEO: I'm just going to - I'll let others
talk about the progress that we're making

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:21.020
there.

00:44:21.020 --> 00:44:22.020
MR. THIESSEN: OK.

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:26.490
MR. POMPEO: But you should know that the CIA
understands that. Our analytic assessment

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:30.890
is much in line with what you describe. It
is an absolute imperative that we achieve

00:44:30.890 --> 00:44:34.109
that if we're going to be successful for the
long run.

00:44:34.109 --> 00:44:37.109
MR. THIESSEN: OK. One last question, then
we're going to turn it to the audience. So

00:44:37.109 --> 00:44:42.260
this is a question I ask all national security
policymakers when they come to AEI. So during

00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:47.290
the 1988 presidential debates, no one asked
either candidate about Iraq. And during the

00:44:47.290 --> 00:44:51.380
2000 presidential debates, no one asked either
candidate about al Qaeda. Yet in both those

00:44:51.380 --> 00:44:58.890
cases, those two elements because crises that
became to dominate the presidencies of George

00:44:58.890 --> 00:45:04.200
H. W. Bush and George W. Bush. What is the
threat that's out there - and you see these

00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:08.290
- you see threats none of us see, but what
is the thing that worries you the most in

00:45:08.290 --> 00:45:12.000
the world that none of us are talking about
and none of these journalists here are asking

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:18.930
anyone about that really could come out and
surprise us - that you worry about at night?

00:45:18.930 --> 00:45:24.579
MR. POMPEO: The list is long. The list is
long. So we have these set of things that

00:45:24.579 --> 00:45:28.869
would be in the national intelligence priorities
framework, and we would all know them. They'd

00:45:28.869 --> 00:45:35.099
be in the news many days. Then there's a set
of other things that one shouldn't characterize

00:45:35.099 --> 00:45:43.260
as second tier in spite of the fact that they're
not in the news each day. Certainly the political

00:45:43.260 --> 00:45:49.020
risks in South America are one of them - to
make sure we get that right and that the United

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:55.059
States is watching what's taking place in,
frankly, every place south of our border - Mexico,

00:45:55.059 --> 00:45:57.369
Central America, and South America.

00:45:57.369 --> 00:46:03.050
And then the other thing that - frankly, that
the Intelligence Committee needs to get right

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:08.860
is that some of these threats that are real
- one of them you mentioned, al Qaeda, aren't

00:46:08.860 --> 00:46:13.950
nation-states. So, historically, the threats
to the United States, and your - I'm looking

00:46:13.950 --> 00:46:17.880
around the room to see how old everybody is
- your history books would have all reflected

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:24.150
threats from countries. What was America doing
against the threat from Yugoslavia or some

00:46:24.150 --> 00:46:29.450
other nation-state? Today, the threats are
much more varied than that, whether it's threats

00:46:29.450 --> 00:46:35.770
from groups like Hezbollah or al Qaeda, threats
to our information systems from groups like

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:42.170
Wikileaks. They don't have a flag at the UN,
and they present real threats to the United

00:46:42.170 --> 00:46:43.170
States of America.

00:46:43.170 --> 00:46:47.869
And so we need to make sure that our collection
- the way we think about attacking our adversaries

00:46:47.869 --> 00:46:51.550
from an intelligence perspective - matches
that. And that means we have to go back and

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:57.110
fix some of the rules, some of the laws that
are designed to solve the nation-state challenge

00:46:57.110 --> 00:46:58.110
of history.

00:46:58.110 --> 00:47:03.329
Cyber is another vector. It's not a threat
of its own, but it is a means by which many

00:47:03.329 --> 00:47:07.850
non-nation-state actors can inflict incredible
costs on the United States of America. We

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:12.170
need to make sure that we are watching those
actors in the same way we would a threat from

00:47:12.170 --> 00:47:13.280
a traditional nation-state.

00:47:13.280 --> 00:47:18.000
MR. THIESSEN: All right. Let's take some questions
from the audience. Ambassador Roger Noriega.

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:21.359
There's a microphone coming to you.

00:47:21.359 --> 00:47:27.710
Q: Good morning. Happy to see you again.

00:47:27.710 --> 00:47:34.849
MR. POMPEO: Hi, Roger. Good to see you, too.

00:47:34.849 --> 00:47:38.990
Q: One of the unconventional threats that
we're confronting is transnational organized

00:47:38.990 --> 00:47:44.150
crime. I know I've been talking to you about
this for a good long while. In the year that

00:47:44.150 --> 00:47:50.630
you've been director, have you been - has
it been a priority of yours to put more intelligence

00:47:50.630 --> 00:47:57.820
resources and going after the financial network
of drug traffickers and other forms of transnational

00:47:57.820 --> 00:48:05.180
organized crime, which is now playing an active
role in the destruction of Venezuela and looking

00:48:05.180 --> 00:48:10.830
at influencing elections in Columbia and Mexico,
where drug traffickers have a significant

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:18.200
interest in sowing mayhem and optimizing supply
chain of cocaine to the market here in the

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:19.200
United States?

00:48:19.200 --> 00:48:22.869
MR. POMPEO: You could probably add others
to the list. I would list the Taliban as well,

00:48:22.869 --> 00:48:31.020
who uses drug revenue to foment so much pain
on the world. We have bolstered our capacity

00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:36.760
there - our collection capacity. We've done
that jointly with the Treasury Department,

00:48:36.760 --> 00:48:41.720
with Secretary Mnuchin and his team, working
together to take the tradecraft that we have

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:46.400
historically used against networks that look
and feel very much like financial networks

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:48.640
and applied them against those very same networks.

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:56.049
There's still a long way to go, but what it
delivers for policymakers is a set of options.

00:48:56.049 --> 00:49:02.569
Publicly what you'd see would be sanctions
options, but in other spaces it presents other

00:49:02.569 --> 00:49:06.550
ways that we can disrupt because if we know
where the money is, we have a capability to

00:49:06.550 --> 00:49:11.920
stop that flow. We're probably still not at
the level we need to be, but we are certainly

00:49:11.920 --> 00:49:15.431
in a better place when we were just a short
time ago.

00:49:15.431 --> 00:49:21.330
Q: I just want to note that one of the challenges
will be the recovery of assets. The Maduro

00:49:21.330 --> 00:49:28.420
regime has looted about $350 billion from
Venezuela. Being able to recover those assets

00:49:28.420 --> 00:49:32.990
and repurposing and giving them back to the
Venezuelan people to reconstruct their country

00:49:32.990 --> 00:49:33.990
would be a priority, too.

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:36.980
MR. POMPEO: Thank you. There are more than
half a dozen places in the world that I can

00:49:36.980 --> 00:49:45.420
tell you we are watching large amounts of
wealth that have been stolen from the people

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:52.049
of that country and our effort to identify
them, gain the capacity to take them away

00:49:52.049 --> 00:49:58.420
from the person who has title to them today,
and then use them for US foreign policy purposes

00:49:58.420 --> 00:50:05.300
- frankly, in most cases to try and return
them to the people from which they were looted.

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:08.700
MR. THIESSEN: Nick Eberstadt.

00:50:08.700 --> 00:50:14.560
Q: Director Pompeo, how satisfied were you
with the intelligence capabilities on North

00:50:14.560 --> 00:50:20.520
Korea that you inherited when you came to
Langley? What do you see as the unknowables?

00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:25.480
What do you see as the things that are knowable,
but we don't know about and should?

00:50:25.480 --> 00:50:31.480
MR. POMPEO: So when I came in, there was insufficient
focus on the problem set. It wasn't the case

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:36.900
that it had been ignored. It wasn't the case
that we had missed material things, but clearly

00:50:36.900 --> 00:50:41.080
hadn't received the focus and attention that
were going to be needed to deliver for what

00:50:41.080 --> 00:50:47.200
this administration is going to ask of the
intelligence community. So we - within weeks

00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:51.500
of me coming here, I created a Korean mission
center, stood it up with a senior leader who

00:50:51.500 --> 00:50:58.559
had just retired, brought him back to run
the organization that now numbers - I can

00:50:58.559 --> 00:51:02.410
see my information security people. There's
a lot of folks working on it. (Laughter.)

00:51:02.410 --> 00:51:11.089
We're in a much better place today than we
were 12 months ago. We are still suffering

00:51:11.089 --> 00:51:14.359
from having gaps. Part of that is not the
intelligence community's fault per se. These

00:51:14.359 --> 00:51:18.170
are the difficult target sets. I'll concede
that at the outset.

00:51:18.170 --> 00:51:22.180
But it's never - it's completely inadequate
for the CIA to say, well, that's a hard problem.

00:51:22.180 --> 00:51:27.990
Of course it's a hard problem. That's why
you pay us. And so we're making real progress

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:32.750
at developing a global intelligence picture
so that we can get some of those gaps filled

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:36.670
in so that we can understand rates of change,
so that we can understand what's happening

00:51:36.670 --> 00:51:41.690
among the various leadership elements inside
of North Korea, so we can also see whether

00:51:41.690 --> 00:51:46.050
the sanctions that have been put in place
are actually having an effect or sufficient

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:52.579
effect and an effect on whom; that is, which
sets of people inside of North Korea are being

00:51:52.579 --> 00:51:57.339
impact and who the scofflaws are that are
preventing those things from being as effective

00:51:57.339 --> 00:51:59.450
as they ostensibly ought to be.

00:51:59.450 --> 00:52:05.160
There is enormous pressure that has been placed
on me. And, in turn, I have placed enormous

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:10.770
pressure on our team to solve the riddle,
to close each of those gaps to the maximum

00:52:10.770 --> 00:52:13.240
extent we possibly can.

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:16.570
MR. THIESSEN: Martha.

00:52:16.570 --> 00:52:23.420
Q: Hi. I want to go back to - good to see
you. I want to go back to what you said in

00:52:23.420 --> 00:52:29.369
the beginning, that North Korea is a handful
of months away. I assume you mean having a

00:52:29.369 --> 00:52:34.290
nuclear-tipped missile that could possibly
reach the United States. And you said you

00:52:34.290 --> 00:52:40.020
hope we're in the same position a year from
now - that they're a few months away. Is that

00:52:40.020 --> 00:52:45.960
good enough that in a year from now they might
still be a few months away from developing

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:46.960
that weapon?

00:52:46.960 --> 00:52:48.950
MR. POMPEO: No.

00:52:48.950 --> 00:52:52.380
Q: OK. So what did you mean by that, exactly?

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:58.599
MR. POMPEO: The president - that's inconsistent
with US policy, right? US policy is that we're

00:52:58.599 --> 00:53:04.750
going to denuclearize permanently, right,
that we're going to foreclose this risk. But

00:53:04.750 --> 00:53:09.820
it's the case, in the event we haven't gotten
there, it is still a secondary mission to

00:53:09.820 --> 00:53:15.010
ensure that we keep them from having that
capability. And I want to say this, too: We

00:53:15.010 --> 00:53:21.180
often focus on timelines because it's simple.
It's not the way we ought to think about it,

00:53:21.180 --> 00:53:24.580
right? The way we ought to be think about
it is reliability.

00:53:24.580 --> 00:53:30.300
Can they reliably deliver the pain which Kim
Jong Un wants to be able to deliver against

00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:36.051
the United States of America? It's one thing
to be able to say, yes, it's possible. You

00:53:36.051 --> 00:53:40.890
could - if everything went right, if the missile
flew in the right direction and we got lucky,

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:47.480
we could do it, as opposed to certainty, right?
This is the core of deterrence theory. You

00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:51.559
have to be certain. In the deterrence model,
you have to be certain that what you aim to

00:53:51.559 --> 00:53:55.829
deliver will actually be successful. At the
very least, you need to make sure your adversary

00:53:55.829 --> 00:53:58.710
believes that - that it is certain.

00:53:58.710 --> 00:54:03.849
That's what Kim Jong Un is driving for. He
is trying to put in our mind the reality that

00:54:03.849 --> 00:54:08.050
he can deliver that pain to the United States
of America, and our mission is to make the

00:54:08.050 --> 00:54:10.819
day that he can do that as far off as possible.

00:54:10.819 --> 00:54:16.579
Q: And can I just follow up to the intel question,
and you said a couple of months away. We know

00:54:16.579 --> 00:54:22.560
the intelligence before had it far in the
future - six months, two years, whatever,

00:54:22.560 --> 00:54:28.220
and he was much faster than you thought. So
do you trust - do you feel you have enough

00:54:28.220 --> 00:54:35.599
information in saying a couple of months away
to be certain? And also on the sanctions,

00:54:35.599 --> 00:54:37.920
you said that's a gap, essentially, just now
-

00:54:37.920 --> 00:54:39.920
MR. POMPEO: The impact of sanctions. Yes.
Yes, ma'am.

00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:45.510
Q: The impact - the impact of sanctions. So
do you see any impact now that you trusted

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:48.160
also on the missile timeline?

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:52.500
MR. POMPEO: So I can't answer your second
question, so I'll do my best to answer your

00:54:52.500 --> 00:54:56.840
first. It's not that I don't know. I do know.
I just can't share with you what we know on

00:54:56.840 --> 00:54:57.960
the second question.

00:54:57.960 --> 00:55:03.150
The first one, your predicate's actually wrong.
You said that somehow that the intelligence

00:55:03.150 --> 00:55:07.799
community got this wrong. We didn't see this
coming as fast as - that's just untrue. I've

00:55:07.799 --> 00:55:11.710
seen the news articles that have written that.
By the way, it's not me bragging. This all

00:55:11.710 --> 00:55:17.289
happened before my time, all of this work.
The Intelligence Committee on this one actually

00:55:17.289 --> 00:55:23.200
understood the capability and the testing
capacity. We'll never get the week or the

00:55:23.200 --> 00:55:27.480
month right on something that's this complicated.
But we can get the direction of travel and

00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:30.630
the capacity for rate of change right, and
we did.

00:55:30.630 --> 00:55:35.859
We believe we are continuing to deliver good,
solid information on the North Korean missile

00:55:35.859 --> 00:55:40.950
testing program and all the ancillary pieces
around it. We're pretty confident in that.

00:55:40.950 --> 00:55:46.540
MR. THIESSEN: Let's take one more question.
In the front here. Wait for the microphone

00:55:46.540 --> 00:55:47.540
please.

00:55:47.540 --> 00:55:56.580
Q: Thank you, sir. I'm Janne Kum Pak with
the USA Journal. Recently, North Korea and

00:55:56.580 --> 00:56:05.290
Kim Jong Un wants a dialogue with South Korea.
On the other hand, Kim Jong Un's nuclear threat

00:56:05.290 --> 00:56:13.680
- continues to threat the United States with
nuclear and missiles. What is the US final

00:56:13.680 --> 00:56:23.190
destination of resolve of the North Korean
nuclear issues, and is it possible to preemptive

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:25.890
strike to North Korea if necessary?

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:32.970
MR. POMPEO: So I'll leave to others to address
the capacity or the wisdom of preemptive strike.

00:56:32.970 --> 00:56:37.309
From an intelligence perspective, we're trying
to ensure that all the various options that

00:56:37.309 --> 00:56:41.780
the president might want to consider are fully
informed and we understand what's really going

00:56:41.780 --> 00:56:46.299
on and the risks associated with each of those
decisions as best we can identify them for

00:56:46.299 --> 00:56:47.299
him.

00:56:47.299 --> 00:56:51.750
Let me just sort of - we're doing that in
conjunction too, right? We should remember

00:56:51.750 --> 00:56:55.400
we have partners there that are working on
this diligently as well - the South Koreans

00:56:55.400 --> 00:57:00.900
themselves, the Japanese. We have partners
throughout the region that share our understanding

00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:05.770
that this is a global threat. We often here
in the States talk about the threat here.

00:57:05.770 --> 00:57:08.260
This is a threat to the whole world.

00:57:08.260 --> 00:57:12.960
MR. THIESSEN: OK. Ladies and gentlemen, we
have to end it here. I ask everybody to stay

00:57:12.960 --> 00:57:17.660
seated while the director leaves. And, Director
Pompeo, I know you don't do a lot of these

00:57:17.660 --> 00:57:20.839
things. We're hugely honored that you came
here to AEI to spend some time with us, and

00:57:20.839 --> 00:57:22.170
thank you very much for your service to the
country.

00:57:22.170 --> 00:57:22.810
MR. POMPEO: Thank you, Marc.

